portant nuance: not all stage IV cancers are necessarily metastatic, so “stage IV” and “metastatic” are related but not synonymous. [Data: Entities (857); Relationships (2743, 2744)]\\n\\nMetastatic cancer is separately described as an advanced stage of cancer in which malignant cells have spread from the primary site to distant parts of the body, and it is also described using TNM staging (particularly the “M” category) to guide prognosis and treatment planning. Taken together, the dataset presents overlapping terminology (metastatic disease vs metastatic cancer) while preserving clinical nuance about staging labels versus the biological/clinical reality of spread. [Data: Entities (971, 857)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community links metastatic classification to modern treatment selection via expanded biomarker testing\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Expanded biomarker testing is described as additional tumor biomarker evaluation recommended in metastatic cancer to identify actionable/targetable features beyond initial MMR/MSI testing. This is operationalized by an explicit relationship stating that the purpose of expanded biomarker testing is identifying targetable features of the cancer. [Data: Entities (4426, 4427); Relationships (9310)]\\n\\nThis positions metastatic status not only as a prognostic/staging milestone but also as a trigger for deeper molecular characterization to inform therapy selection. In the dataset’s metastatic cancer description, management considerations include systemic therapy and expanded biomarker testing to guide selection (including potential immune checkpoint inhibitors for appropriate biomarker profiles), aligning the community’s “spread” concepts with biomarker-driven decision pathways. [Data: Entities (971, 4426, 4427); Relationships (9310)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 7.6,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity is high because the community describes advanced, system-wide cancer spread (metastasis) tied